<!-- A front node (conf/front/README.md; plans/Edge-Node2.md §4.3, §4.7): the front option set alone, node_role front.
    It faces browsers and balances the nodes behind it, which pair with it and dial it: web access by host name
    (terminate or passthrough, with the client's address passed on), TCP ports sent to a pairing, load balancing over a
    pairing's members with sticky sessions and health, web protection, TLS and certificates, and the offline page. It
    has none of the edge set: no mail, call media, TURN, SIP, XMPP server, queue, delegated calls, replication or
    mr-pass, and it starts none of them even with their ports set. Its database holds only its pairings, host names,
    ports and certificates; it holds no business data.
    It is deployed two ways, with this conf for both: before a local cluster (conf/front, each member running
    conf/front/MoquiLocalConf.xml), for load balancing and web protection with no edge node; and before an edge cluster
    (conf/full-stack), whose members are its pool and forward to their local nodes.
    Build, archive and run with the release flag of ./moq: Maximal (embedded Postgres is enough), or Enterprise with an
    external database. The runtime needs the base components only. Per-host settings go in .env. -->
<moqui-conf xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://moqui.org/xsd/moqui-conf-3.xsd">
    <default-property name="instance_purpose" value="production"/>
    <!-- takes links from the nodes behind it on the HTTPS listener, told from browsers by ALPN; its Hello names the role
        front and offers web access and ports only, so a node pairing with it keeps it as its front pairing
        (front_node_url, front_node_key, front_node_pin and front_node_id) and the wizard there shows only those.
        System › Edge Clients makes pairing codes and shows the pairings, their members and names. A front node dials no
        other node. -->
    <default-property name="node_role" value="front"/>
    <!-- rate limits, connection limits and bans by client address, run before anything goes down a link: the nodes
        behind it see the client's address, and a ban they start (failed sign-ins) is told up the link, so this node
        refuses that client for the pairing's own names (System › Security › Web Protection). With a proxy before this
        node, name it in webapp_trusted_proxies (.env): a client-ip-header with no trusted proxies is ignored. -->
    <default-property name="webapp_web_protection" value="true"/>

    <!-- The link and web access (.env): webapp_http_host, this node's own public name, where nodes pair and its System
        app is. The HTTPS listener needs a WebPKI certificate in runtime/cert for it, which a node checks when it pairs;
        no TLS-terminating proxy before this node, since the links need ALPN and client certificates end to end. Bind
        443 (docs/Run-Deploy.md, Listen ports and URL ports): the listener binds webapp_https_listen_port, 8443 by
        default. The links use this node's own key, edge_link_key, generated into .env at the first start; a changed key
        means every node behind it must pair again. edge_public_addresses: this node's public IPv4 and IPv6 addresses,
        comma-separated, which the wizard checks the host names' A and AAAA records against.
        A host name's mode is set by the node behind that gives it. Passthrough: this node reads the name before any TLS
        and passes the connection down a member's link with a PROXY header; the member ends the TLS with its own
        certificate, and this node sees nothing of the content. Terminate: this node ends the TLS with its certificate
        for the name and forwards each request, so it sees the session cookie and keeps a browser on one member; while
        no member is linked it shows an offline page (503), the page at edge_offline_page when set. Certificates for
        terminate names go in runtime/cert, or System › Certificates sets a name up with ACME (TLS-ALPN-01): a
        passthrough name's challenge goes down the tunnel to the node that ends its TLS. -->

    <!-- Ports (.env and rows): edge_ssh_tunnel_port (and edge_ssh_tunnel_host) takes SSH connections for the one active
        pairing's SSH server; with several pairings each gets a port of its own, a moqui.edge.EdgePort row (System › Edge
        Clients › Ports). The XMPP ports, for every pairing's XMPP domain by its name:
                                     <default-property name="edge_xmpp_port" value="5222"/>
                                     <default-property name="edge_xmpp_tls_port" value="5223"/>
        (and edge_xmpp_host). Mail: port 25 as an EdgePort row of kind smtp (only on a front node; on edge_smtp_tunnel_host,
        every address when empty; at most 128 connections at once), each connection passed to the SMTP listener of a member of that pairing's pool with a PROXY header
        naming the client, which ends the STARTTLS and makes every check itself: before an edge cluster its members,
        the mail edge (conf/full-stack); before a local cluster its members, when they take mail from the internet
        themselves (conf/front/MoquiLocalConf.xml). Each port is balanced over the pairing's live members, sticky by the
        client's address. -->

    <!-- Not here: call media, TURN and SIP never go through a front. Calls and phones reach the edge members' own
        addresses (conf/full-stack), or a local cluster's own media nodes. -->

    <!-- Web protection's rules per host (plans/Edge-Node2.md §4.3): a rate-limit, client-rule, path-rule or
        user-agent-rule with host applies to that host's requests on top of the rules without one, so one site can be
        stricter than another. In the webroot webapp of this conf, for example:
            <web-protection>
                <rate-limit name="shop-checkout" host="shop.example.com" path="/checkout/**" methods="POST"
                        requests="5" seconds="60" burst="10"/>
                <path-rule name="forum-admin" host="forum.example.org" action="deny" path="/admin/**"/>
            </web-protection>
        Bans count every host's events and are shared with this node's cluster members. -->

    <!-- Backups: this node's pairings, host names, ports and ACME certificates are rows; losing them means pairing
        every node again. The nightly backup_EmbeddedDatabase_daily job is paused in the seed data; this sets it running
        at each boot (docs/Run-Deploy.md, Backups: its mirror, keeping a copy off this host). It backs up the embedded
        database only: back an external one up with its own tools. -->
    <default-property name="embedded_db_backup_scheduled" value="true"/>

    <entity-facade crypt-pass="${entity_ds_crypt_pass}" query-stats="true"/>

    <!-- Balancing a pairing's pool (plans/Edge-Node2.md §4.4): a terminate host's requests stay on the member their
        session cookie hashes to (sticky-cookie, the session cookie; a host's own on Edge Clients › Domains), a
        passthrough host's connections and the ports' on the member of the client's address; a member whose requests
        fail or time out set-aside-failures times in a row gets no new work for set-aside-seconds. A front keeps no
        queue, so the queue's attributes are not used. Several front nodes are a cluster of their own: the same .env on
        one external database, each with its own edge_link_url, and every node behind them links to each member. -->
    <edge sticky-cookie="moqui.session" set-aside-failures="3" set-aside-seconds="30"/>
</moqui-conf>
